  "_ONEOFF_CARDS": [
    "The four full-frame pictures behind the game's one-off moments. Each one replaced a",
    "single draw_rect of near-black with centred text on it - see scripts/pixelise_paint.py's",
    "UI_ONEOFFS note for why these four and not others. Delete a line and that screen goes",
    "back to its flat fill, which is still the code's fallback path.",
    "",
    "They share one composition rule that a backdrop does not have: the type runs straight",
    "down the MIDDLE, roughly y 14%..88%, and each screen finishes with draw_vignette(s, 0.7),",
    "a 170px band at every edge. So the picture has to be dark and quiet through the centre",
    "and cannot put its detail hard against the edge either."
  ],
  "_GEAR_ICONS": [
    "The wardrobe rack's eight pieces. AUTHORED WHITE/GREYSCALE ON PURPOSE - draw_gear_icon",
    "blits these through a TINT, and the tint is carrying information at both call sites: the",
    "gear room passes the piece's own e[\"col\"] (so the Helmet comes out orange and the Glow",
    "Suit green), and the tailor passes a row STATE (cream selected, dimmer owned, dimmest",
    "not). Painting colour in would fight both. They store a luminance RATIO normalised so the",
    "brightest pixel is white - the same rule the hands and body regions follow, because a",
    "tint can only ever darken. See scripts/install_gear_icons.py.",
    "",
    "A RENDERED GARMENT DOES NOT SURVIVE 20x20 AND A PICTOGRAM DOES. The coat, waistcoat and",
    "gloves were generated first as shaded objects like the rest, and all three reduced to",
    "unreadable mush while the hat, boots, glasses and helmet came through fine - a hat is a",
    "silhouette, a tailcoat is detail. Re-asked as flat high-contrast SHOP PICTOGRAMS (thick",
    "chunky shapes, no texture, no small detail) all three read immediately. Worth knowing",
    "before generating anything else this small."
  ],
